* sqlite3 shell
@code bash
sqlite3 database.sqlite
@end

* List tables
@code sql
.tables
@end

* Show schema
@code sql
.schema
.schema users
@end

* Create table
@code sql
CREATE TABLE users (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
    email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
    name TEXT,
    created_at TEXT DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);
@end

* Insert rows
@code sql
INSERT INTO users (email, name)
VALUES ('viktor@example.com', 'Viktor');
@end

* Query rows
@code sql
SELECT * FROM users;
SELECT id, email FROM users WHERE email LIKE '%@example.com';
@end

* Update rows
@code sql
UPDATE users
SET name = 'Viktor Nagy'
WHERE id = 1;
@end

* Delete rows
@code sql
DELETE FROM users
WHERE id = 1;
@end

* Add a column
@code sql
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN is_active INTEGER DEFAULT 1;
@end

* Dump database
@code bash
sqlite3 database.sqlite .dump > dump.sql
@end

* Import dump
@code bash
sqlite3 new.sqlite < dump.sql
@end

* Nice output in shell
@code sql
.mode column
.headers on
SELECT * FROM users;
@end

* Back up a live database
@code bash
sqlite3 database.sqlite ".backup 'backup.sqlite'"
@end
Use SQLite's backup command rather than copying only the main file while writes or WAL mode may be active.

* Restore into a separate database file
@code bash
sqlite3 restored.sqlite ".restore 'backup.sqlite'"
sqlite3 restored.sqlite 'PRAGMA integrity_check;'
@end
Choose a new destination filename. `integrity_check` should return `ok`; also check the tables and data you need.

* Preview an update in a transaction
Run inside the sqlite3 shell:
@code sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE users SET name = 'Example' WHERE id = 1;
SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE id = 1;
ROLLBACK;
@end
Replace `ROLLBACK;` with `COMMIT;` only when the change should persist.

Reference: {https://www.sqlite.org/cli.html}[sqlite3 shell].
